186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1039 "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186114919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115356 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1045 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d07d2d5c-d3e0-595b-84ff-e18597a3d040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1045 "The Domain of a Vector-Valued Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115482 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ec8f8b4c-f624-58ca-8d08-26dd958651aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\nII. $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n\nIII. The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$ intersect at infinitely many points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07b54935-f6c1-5cc5-a220-23bab58f39a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115884 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot k-2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6k+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $6k+6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1061 16471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "836cc4ee-5cd5-58a8-8fc6-487e670dc062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1061 16472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33db29bf-42ca-5174-b04b-c5529dc2bc98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1061 16473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a71d8e7-c420-59a3-927f-619b1fbcc37e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116113 #edb/ref 1061 16474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116113 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72cfa9ea-e123-5f2c-bad3-38a7904ca9f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116113 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1061 44256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c2c6552-011f-50fb-b06a-06bc2262ccf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116304 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1061 44257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a07d4a92-4626-5d50-92cc-ea8d55c5707d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116305 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1061 44297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cfc9c5f1-5579-598f-a9e2-d23b659763f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116306 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116307 #edb/ref 1061 44801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116307 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3947d2bf-1e5f-5028-8a1b-43cfaacc4546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116307 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n- $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n- The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n- The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1065 3050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5a0ce34c-f39a-5c24-bab3-840ff974402c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
